1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,810
Roman Britain, over 1,500 years ago: Silvianus loses a gold ring.

2
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:09,973
So he curses the thief. On a sheet of lead.

3
00:00:10,013 --> 00:00:15,706
Centuries later: a gold ring near Silchester, carved with the suspect's name.

4
00:00:15,746 --> 00:00:23,419
"The real One Ring"? Did Tolkien make it all up? No. But he didn't copy it either.

5
00:00:23,459 --> 00:00:27,916
The real stories behind Middle-earth, and the truth about that ring.

6
00:00:30,316 --> 00:00:45,750
Tolkien was a philologist: a scholar of old languages.

7
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:54,569
Oxford English Dictionary, then (1925) Oxford professor of Anglo-Saxon.

8
00:00:54,609 --> 00:01:03,153
1926: he meets C.S. Lewis. The Inklings read their drafts aloud.

9
00:01:03,193 --> 00:01:13,085
A Finnish grammar: "like discovering a complete wine-cellar".

10
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:22,614
Finnish shaped Quenya. Sindarin was built to sound like Welsh.

11
00:01:22,654 --> 00:01:28,836
Kullervo, from the Kalevala: "the germ" of his legends.

12
00:01:28,876 --> 00:01:34,895
1913-14: one line in the Old English poem Crist.

13
00:01:34,935 --> 00:01:40,408
"Hail Earendel, brightest of angels, over middle-earth sent to men."

14
00:01:40,448 --> 00:01:47,845
Sept 1914: "The Voyage of Earendel the Evening Star".

15
00:01:47,885 --> 00:01:58,433
"Middle-earth" = Old English middangeard, cousin of Norse Midgard.

16
00:01:58,473 --> 00:02:03,592
The name came from a poem. The darkness came from somewhere worse.

17
00:02:06,392 --> 00:02:16,932
June 1916: Lt Tolkien, signals officer. Telephones, lamps, pigeons.

18
00:02:16,972 --> 00:02:27,344
1 July 1916: 57,470 British casualties in one day. 19,240 killed.

19
00:02:27,384 --> 00:02:33,782
His battalion was in reserve. Two weeks later, he attacked.

20
00:02:33,822 --> 00:02:40,907
141 days. Over a million casualties, all sides.

21
00:02:40,947 --> 00:02:48,438
His school club, the T.C.B.S. Rob Gilson: killed on day one.

22
00:02:48,478 --> 00:02:53,299
22 July 1916: Ralph Payton, killed.

23
00:02:53,339 --> 00:02:59,579
December 1916: Geoffrey Smith dies of his wounds.

24
00:02:59,619 --> 00:03:05,825
"May you say the things I have tried to say long after I am not there to say them."

25
00:03:05,865 --> 00:03:12,975
Trench fever sent him home in Nov 1916. He never went back.

26
00:03:13,015 --> 00:03:16,225
Trench warfare: "animal horror".

27
00:03:16,265 --> 00:03:22,533
"By 1918 all but one of my close friends were dead."

28
00:03:22,573 --> 00:03:34,057
On sick leave: The Fall of Gondolin. The Dead Marshes "owe something" to the Somme.

29
00:03:34,097 --> 00:03:42,916
Sam: a reflection of the English privates and batmen he knew.

30
00:03:42,956 --> 00:03:52,065
Not an allegory, he insisted. What haunted him was a wave.

31
00:03:54,865 --> 00:04:02,309
To W.H. Auden (1955): "I have what some might call an Atlantis complex."

32
00:04:02,349 --> 00:04:09,422
"the terrible recurrent dream ... of the Great Wave"

33
00:04:09,462 --> 00:04:12,776
"I bequeathed it to Faramir."

34
00:04:12,816 --> 00:04:24,582
Plato: Atlantis tries to conquer Athens, and loses.

35
00:04:24,622 --> 00:04:29,043
"one grievous day and night": swallowed by the sea.

36
00:04:29,083 --> 00:04:37,403
Tolkien's drowned island: Numenor, "the Land in the West". A key Rings of Power setting.

37
00:04:37,443 --> 00:04:44,052
Its fall: Akallabeth / Atalantie, both meaning "Downfall".

38
00:04:44,092 --> 00:04:50,488
The dream stopped after he wrote the "Downfall of Numenor".

39
00:04:50,528 --> 00:05:01,503
Tolkien: "a curious chance" that it resembles Atlantis.

40
00:05:01,543 --> 00:05:13,891
Pride, and forbidden power. And magic rings are far older than Tolkien.

41
00:05:16,691 --> 00:05:24,612
Ring 1: Plato's Ring of Gyges makes its wearer invisible.

42
00:05:24,652 --> 00:05:33,356
Gyges takes a kingdom. Plato asks: would anyone stay just if invisible?

43
00:05:33,396 --> 00:05:41,753
A parallel, NOT a proven source.

44
00:05:41,793 --> 00:05:52,736
Ring 2: the dwarf Andvari's ring, Volsunga saga.

45
00:05:52,776 --> 00:06:02,480
Andvari curses it. Tolkien retold the tale in verse (Sigurd and Gudrun).

46
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:09,558
Ring 3: Wagner's Ring cycle, from the same legends.

47
00:06:09,598 --> 00:06:18,442
Tolkien (1961): "Both rings were round, and there the resemblance ceases."

48
00:06:18,482 --> 00:06:26,463
Scholars still argue about Wagner.

49
00:06:26,503 --> 00:06:35,962
Cursed gold needs a guard.

50
00:06:38,762 --> 00:06:46,374
Beowulf: a thief steals one cup from a dragon's hoard.

51
00:06:46,414 --> 00:06:51,564
The dragon wakes, and burns the land.

52
00:06:51,604 --> 00:06:57,722
The Hobbit: Bilbo steals a cup from Smaug.

53
00:06:57,762 --> 00:07:04,743
Sept 1937: The Hobbit. First printing sold out in ~3 months.

54
00:07:04,783 --> 00:07:09,205
The Lord of the Rings: about 12 years to write (1954-55).

55
00:07:09,245 --> 00:07:17,396
Not "consciously present", but "among my most valued sources".

56
00:07:17,436 --> 00:07:27,224
1936 lecture: "Beowulf: The Monsters and the Critics".

57
00:07:27,264 --> 00:07:36,680
His Beowulf translation: finished 1926, published 2014.

58
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:42,395
The dragon: Old English. The dwarves: Old Norse.

59
00:07:45,195 --> 00:07:54,715
Voluspa, Poetic Edda: a list of dwarf names.

60
00:07:54,755 --> 00:08:02,450
Durin, Dvalin, Thorin, Fili, Kili, Gloin... even "Oakenshield".

61
00:08:02,490 --> 00:08:05,315
And on the same list: GANDALF.

62
00:08:05,355 --> 00:08:11,721
Tolkien: the dwarf names and the wizard's are "from the Elder Edda".

63
00:08:11,761 --> 00:08:18,246
Early drafts: "Gandalf" was the chief dwarf's name.

64
00:08:18,286 --> 00:08:26,569
Caution: the dwarf list may be a later addition to the poem.

65
00:08:26,609 --> 00:08:35,226
Durin in The Rings of Power: a name from a Norse manuscript.

66
00:08:35,266 --> 00:08:43,086
And then there's the gold ring.

67
00:08:45,886 --> 00:08:53,493
Silvianus's curse tablet: temple of Nodens, Lydney.

68
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:59,801
"Deny health to those called Senicianus" until it's returned.

69
00:08:59,841 --> 00:09:07,604
Silchester ring (found 1785): "Seniciane vivas in Deo".

70
00:09:07,644 --> 00:09:14,469
A curse naming Senicianus. A ring naming Senicianus.

71
00:09:14,509 --> 00:09:23,007
1928-29: Mortimer Wheeler excavates Lydney.

72
00:09:23,047 --> 00:09:35,675
The expert: Tolkien. His note on "Nodens" printed in 1932.

73
00:09:38,475 --> 00:09:41,735
The real One Ring? NO.

74
00:09:41,775 --> 00:09:54,260
National Trust: "wrongly identified" as the inspiration.

75
00:09:54,300 --> 00:10:01,786
The real "ring": words, a drowned island, a dragon, lost friends.

76
00:10:01,826 --> 00:10:05,897
NEXT: How long would you last in Middle-earth?
